Ansible Vault

Today I learned about Ansible Vault, which is used to secure sensitive data like passwords, API keys, and secrets 🔐
Instead of storing secrets in plain text inside Playbooks, Ansible Vault allows us to encrypt them safely.
 Secure automation = Professional automation 🛡️

🔹 🔒 Encrypt Secrets
Ansible Vault helps encrypt:
 • Passwords
 • API keys
 • Database credentials
 • SSH secrets
This prevents sensitive information from being exposed in Playbooks or repositories.

🔹 🧾 vault create / edit / view
Common Vault commands:
ansible-vault create secrets.yml → Create an encrypted file
 ansible-vault edit secrets.yml → Edit encrypted file securely
 ansible-vault view secrets.yml → View encrypted content
These commands ensure secrets remain protected while still being usable in automation.

🔹 📌 Best Practices
✔ Never store secrets in plain text
 ✔ Use separate vault files for sensitive data
 ✔ Restrict access to vault passwords
 ✔ Use strong encryption passwords
Following best practices improves security in real DevOps environments 🚀

🔹 🛡️ Security Handling
Vault files are encrypted using a password and can be safely committed to version control.
During Playbook execution, Ansible decrypts the data securely using the vault password.

 This ensures:
 • Confidential data protection
 • Secure deployments
 • Compliance with security standards
Vault is essential for production-grade automation environments.

🔥 3-Line Takeaway
Ansible Vault protects sensitive data.
 Encrypt secrets instead of hardcoding them.
 Secure automation is a must in production.
